Given the kernel's default BFQ weight is 100, there's an asymmetry between the minimum and maximum weights ratio to this baseline. Therefore, I think the correct formula should remap only the upper "half" of the range:
```
if (weight <= 100)
    kernel_weight = weight;
else
    kernel_weight = 100 + (weight - 100) / 11;
```
so that units with the default config would still yield the `kernel_weight` of 100. (But at the same time the kernel asymmetry seems a bit arbitrary to me, might be better fixed in BFQ.)

EDIT: Note that the asymmetry is present both in v1 and v2 BFQ cgroup API.

@yuwata I summed the references in [a mail](https://lore.kernel.org/linux-block/20210824105626.GA11367@blackbody.suse.cz/T/#u), plus [these](https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/tree/block/bfq-cgroup.c?h=v5.14-rc7#n1030) [two](https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/tree/block/bfq-cgroup.c?h=v5.14-rc7#n1076).

@poettering The state of things is what it is and I guess it has to be accepted [1]. I'd approach this the similar way how systemd hides v1 vs v2 cgroup API differences and do best effort conversion (to that effect, the alternative to my formula above may be simply a revert of 29eb0eefd14afc9a2424781a28b376db47c3c570).

[1] I'm now referring more to the `io.weight`/`io.bfq.weight` schism, not to the range accepted.
